`Profile::GetFieldIndex` calls `Profile::GetMesg` again internally, so the message table was searched twice per `Field`. This version looks up the message once and scans its fields directly to find the index.

The loop assumes `GetFieldIndex` is a plain linear scan that returns the first index whose field number matches, or `FIT_UINT16_INVALID`. That matches the SDK source as I remember it, but I haven't checked it against this copy.

`profileIndex` is set in the constructor body rather than the initializer list. That way it doesn't matter which of `profile` and `profileIndex` is declared first.

```cpp
Field::Field(const FIT_UINT16 mesgNum, const FIT_UINT8 fieldNum)
    : FieldBase()
    , profile(Profile::GetMesg(mesgNum))
    , profileIndex(FIT_UINT16_INVALID)
    , type(FIT_UINT8_INVALID)
    , isFieldExpanded(FIT_FALSE)
{
    const Profile::MESG* const mesg = profile;
    if (mesg != FIT_NULL)
    {
        const FIT_UINT16 n = mesg->numFields;
        const Profile::FIELD* const fields = mesg->fields;
        for (FIT_UINT16 i = 0; i < n; i++)
        {
            if (fields[i].num == fieldNum)
            {
                profileIndex = i;
                break;
            }
        }
    }
}
```